# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository, and this file is what keeps them that way.
#
# Why it exists: the checkout that produced this file had `core.autocrlf=true` and NO .gitattributes. Under
# that combination Git rewrites line endings on the way in and out, and a shell script that comes back with
# CRLF does not merely look wrong -- bash parses it wrong. The kernel's gate/contract suites run real bash
# (`bash -n gate-*.sh`, and `bash -c ". run/lib.sh ..."`), so a CRLF script turns into "line 1: <log text>:
# No such file or directory" and exit 127, which reads as a broken gate rather than as a packaging accident.
# Everything in run/ was still LF-only on the machine this was written on, so nothing was broken yet; the
# hazard is that nothing was stopping the next checkout from breaking it.
#
# The rules below are deliberately narrow: shell scripts, Gradle scripts, and Java/JSON/properties where a
# stray CR is invisible until something compares bytes. Everything else keeps the platform default, so text
# files that are only ever read by a human or an editor are left alone.

# Shell scripts are executed by bash on Linux and by Git Bash on Windows; both want LF.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.command       text eol=lf

# The Gradle build itself, which shells out to Python and reads path literals.
*.gradle        text eol=lf
*.gradle.kts    text eol=lf
gradlew         text eol=lf
gradle.properties text eol=lf

# Config and data whose bytes are compared by tests or hashed into build stamps.
*.properties    text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.tsv           text eol=lf
*.patch         text eol=lf

# The census tables. They are MEASURED OUTPUT: their bytes are the evidence, a stage records their md5, and a
# checkout that rewrites their line endings makes that md5 unreproducible and makes the next `-Pforbric.censusWrite`
# run look like an edit (measured: a `git stash pop` turned the freshly written 1.21.1 editions CRLF). Narrow on
# purpose: only the two directories that hold them, not *.txt everywhere.
forbric-kernel/src/main/resources/net/forbric/kernel/**     text eol=lf
forbric-kernel/src/main/mc-line/*-tables/**                text eol=lf

# Java sources: javac is indifferent, but byte-for-byte fixtures are not.
*.java          text eol=lf

# Windows launchers must keep CRLF, or cmd.exe mis-parses multi-line batch files.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Never touch these: they are consumed as opaque bytes.
*.jar           binary
*.zip           binary
*.png           binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.so            binary
*.dll           binary
*.dylib         binary
*.class         binary
